summary:
- topic: FY2026 Budget Outlook
  text: Superintendent Matheney presented a difficult budget forecast for FY2026,
    highlighting that maintaining current services would require an 11.4% local budget
    increase. Factors such as expiring federal funds, rising special education needs,
    and reduced philanthropic support will necessitate employee position reductions
    and a non-essential spending freeze.
- topic: SPHS Athletic Complex Referendum
  text: The board held a workshop and extensive discussion on the SPHS Athletic Complex
    improvement project. The administration presented revised costs for a November
    2025 referendum, which were lower than the previously defeated proposal, with
    options for either all-natural grass fields or a mix of synthetic turf and natural
    grass.
- topic: Public Comment on Athletic Complex
  text: Citizens voiced strong opinions on the athletic complex, with many opposing
    artificial turf due to environmental impacts, long-term costs, and the tax burden
    on residents, especially seniors. Speakers repeatedly called for a formal presentation
    from a natural grass field expert to provide a more balanced view.
- topic: Board Vote on Athletic Complex
  text: Following a tense debate, the board passed a substitute motion 4-3 to send
    a two-choice referendum to the city council for the November 2025 ballot. The
    motion strips the project down to the stadium field, track, lighting, and restrooms,
    offering voters a choice between a synthetic turf option (~$5.1M) and a natural
    grass option (~$4.29M).
- topic: PreK Expansion
  text: Assistant Superintendent Dr. Prince announced a partnership with community
    childcare providers to create up to 32 new 'mixed delivery' PreK seats. This expansion,
    which will be presented for board approval in March, comes at no additional cost
    to the district as it is funded by state pass-through dollars.
- topic: Student and Superintendent Reports
  text: Student Representative Angela Kabisa shared updates on student achievements,
    including a new state record in track and field, and school activities for Black
    History Month. Superintendent Matheney celebrated the success of the high school
    basketball teams and reaffirmed the district's commitment to diversity, equity,
    and inclusion.
- topic: Policy Approvals
  text: The board unanimously approved several policies on their second read, including
    the Student Code of Conduct (JIC), Job Descriptions (GAB), School Board Legal
    Status (BB), and Board Member Conflict of Interest (BCB).
